WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.844
EnergyBench is an open benchmark for forecasting on live energy data.

00:00:05.994 --> 00:00:13.206
Forecasts of demand, renewable generation and prices underpin how power systems are operated and traded.

00:00:13.456 --> 00:00:19.856
Time-series foundation models promise accurate forecasts without task-specific training.

00:00:20.106 --> 00:00:25.240
Yet many public benchmarks overlap with the data these models were trained on.

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:36.807
EnergyBench assembles 44 datasets and 1,811 series from system operators, power exchanges and statistical agencies.

00:00:37.057 --> 00:00:47.021
Its fresh track evaluates every model on data published from October 2025 onward, after the training cut-off of the evaluated models.

00:00:48.171 --> 00:00:54.942
Each dataset is scored at two operational horizons, over up to sixty forecast origins.

00:00:55.192 --> 00:01:05.505
Probabilistic and point accuracy are measured by the weighted quantile loss and the mean absolute scaled error, both relative to a seasonal naive forecast.

00:01:06.655 --> 00:01:16.178
24 foundation models are applied zero-shot, alongside 18 deep networks trained on each task and 5 statistical baselines.

00:01:17.328 --> 00:01:23.623
Here, Timestral 209M forecasts a week of German electricity demand.

00:01:23.873 --> 00:01:30.667
The shaded band is its eighty per cent prediction interval; the white line is the realised demand.

00:01:31.817 --> 00:01:45.438
Across all tasks, foundation models lead the probabilistic ranking. The best, Timestral 209M, reduces the weighted quantile loss of the seasonal naive baseline by 43 per cent.

00:01:45.688 --> 00:01:49.708
The strongest task-trained network is WPMixer.

00:01:50.858 --> 00:02:02.633
The advantage of foundation models persists on unseen data: their median relative error is 0.66 on the classic datasets and 0.60 on the fresh track.

00:02:03.783 --> 00:02:15.315
The website lets you inspect individual forecasts, compare any two models task by task, forecast your own series in the browser, and test your own judgement against the models.

00:02:16.465 --> 00:02:22.655
Results, data sources and the full protocol are at energybench.github.io.
